Part I — Mathematical Foundations

1. System Definition

Definition 1.1 (Intelligent Mobile Habitat). An Intelligent Mobile Habitat (IMH) is a tuple:

\mathcal{H} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}(t), \mathcal{S}(t), \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{X}, \mathcal{Y})

where:

· \mathcal{V} = \{N_1, \dots, N_n\} is the set of sensing and actuation nodes,
· \mathcal{E}(t) \subseteq \mathcal{V} \times \mathcal{V} is the time-varying communication graph,
· \mathcal{S}(t) is the global state at time t,
· \mathcal{A} = \{A_1, \dots, A_k\} is the set of specialist agents,
· \mathcal{U} is the control input space,
· \mathcal{X} is the state space,
· \mathcal{Y} is the observation space.

The global state is the collection of all node states:

\mathcal{S}(t) = \{N_i(t) \mid i \in \mathcal{V}\}

Each node N_i is a 12-tuple of state variables:

N_i = (e_i, th_i, bw_i, cp_i, tr_i, bl_i, cf_i, au_i, ia_i, vc_i, q_i, F_i)

where e_i = energy, th_i = thermal state, bw_i = bandwidth, cp_i = compute capacity, tr_i = trust metric, bl_i = belief state, cf_i = confidence, au_i = authority level, ia_i = information age, vc_i = vulnerability count, q_i = queue depth, F_i = fault indicator.

Definition 1.2 (Habitat State Vector). The coupled habitat state is:

X_t = [E_t, W_t, T_t, Q_t, S_t, P_t, R_t, H_t] \in \mathcal{X}

where:

Symbol Subsystem Dimension
E_t Energy \mathbb{R}^m (battery, solar, loads)
W_t Water \mathbb{R}^p (tank levels, quality)
T_t Thermal \mathbb{R}^q (zones, surfaces)
Q_t Air quality \mathbb{R}^r (CO₂, VOC, PM)
S_t Structural \mathbb{R}^s (strain, vibration)
P_t Perception \mathbb{R}^u (environment, obstacles)
R_t Computation \mathbb{R}^v (CPU, memory, queue)
H_t Human context \mathbb{R}^w (occupancy, activity, authority)

Definition 1.3 (Habitat Dynamics). The system evolves according to:

X_{t+1} = f(X_t, U_t, \Theta_t) + \omega_t

where:

· U_t \in \mathcal{U} is the control input (agent actions),
· \Theta_t is the exogenous disturbance (weather, road, environment),
· \omega_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\omega) is process noise.

The observation model is:

Y_t = h(X_t) + \nu_t, \quad \nu_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\nu)

Proposition 1.1. The IMH is a partially observable, stochastic, resource-constrained cyber-physical-human system because:

1. h(\cdot) is not injective → partial observability.
2. \omega_t \neq 0 → stochastic dynamics.
3. \sum_i e_i \leq E_{max} < \infty → energy constraint.
4. H_t introduces non-Markovian human decision dynamics.

Proof. (1) follows from sensor sparsity; (2) from environmental uncertainty; (3) from the finite battery capacity E_{max}; (4) from the fact that human authority allocation (Section 5) depends on latent cognitive states not directly observable. ∎

