2. Multi-Agent Architecture and Emergent Habitat Intelligence

Definition 2.1 (Specialist Agent). A specialist agent A_j is a policy:

A_j: \mathcal{Y}_j \times \mathcal{S}_j \rightarrow \mathcal{U}_j

where \mathcal{Y}_j is the observation subspace and \mathcal{S}_j is the local belief state.

Definition 2.2 (Habitat Intelligence). The Habitat Intelligence \mathcal{I} is the coordination function:

\mathcal{I}: \{\mathcal{U}_1, \dots, \mathcal{U}_k\} \times \mathcal{X} \rightarrow \mathcal{U}

that resolves conflicts between specialist agents.

Theorem 2.1 (Emergent Coordination). If each specialist agent A_j optimizes a local objective J_j subject to shared resource constraints, then the coordination policy \mathcal{I}^* that minimizes the global Lagrangian:

\mathcal{L} = \sum_j \alpha_j J_j + \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}} \mu_c g_c(\mathcal{U})

achieves Pareto optimality in the resource allocation among agents.

Proof. The Lagrangian \mathcal{L} is convex in \mathcal{U} if each J_j is convex and each constraint g_c is affine or convex. The Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions:

\nabla_{\mathcal{U}} \mathcal{L} = 0, \quad \mu_c g_c(\mathcal{U}) = 0, \quad \mu_c \geq 0

define the optimal coordination. Since the feasible set is compact (finite resources) and non-empty (there exists at least one feasible allocation), the KKT conditions are necessary and sufficient. The resulting allocation is Pareto optimal: no agent can improve without degrading another. ∎

Corollary 2.1 (Graceful Degradation). If any subset of agents fails, the remaining agents can maintain a feasible (possibly suboptimal) allocation provided the constraint set remains non-empty.

Proof. The feasible set \mathcal{F} = \{\mathcal{U} : g_c(\mathcal{U}) \leq 0\} is defined by resource constraints. If agents fail, the effective \mathcal{U} is restricted, but as long as \mathcal{U}_{restricted} \cap \mathcal{F} \neq \emptyset, a feasible solution exists. The system degrades to a lower-performance operating point. ∎

