4. Structural Health Monitoring: Multimodal Fusion

Definition 4.1 (Structural State). The structural state S_t is estimated from heterogeneous measurements:

S_t = \Phi(a_t, \epsilon_t, v_t, I_t)

where:

· a_t = acceleration (IMU),
· \epsilon_t = strain (strain gauges),
· v_t = visual motion (camera),
· I_t = environmental loading (wind, thermal).

Definition 4.2 (Strain Frequency Response Function). For a multi-degree-of-freedom system, the Strain Frequency Response Function (SFRF) matrix is:

\mathbf{H}^\epsilon(\omega) = \sum_{r=1}^{m} \frac{\gamma_r \{\psi_r^\epsilon\}\{\phi_r\}^T}{\omega_r^2 - \omega^2 + 2j\zeta_r \omega_r \omega}

where \gamma_r is the modal participation factor, \psi_r^\epsilon is the strain mode shape, \phi_r is the displacement mode shape.

Theorem 4.1 (Damage Detection via Singular Value Decomposition). Define the Reserved Singular Values Mean Change Ratio (RSVMCR):

\text{RSVMCR} = \frac{\bar{\sigma}_{damaged}}{\bar{\sigma}_{healthy}}

where \bar{\sigma} are the reserved singular values of the SFRF matrix. Then:

\text{RSVMCR} < 1 - \delta \implies \text{Damage present}

for a threshold \delta > 0 determined by noise levels.

Proof. Damage reduces local stiffness, which shifts natural frequencies \omega_r and alters mode shapes \phi_r. The SFRF matrix \mathbf{H}^\epsilon is a function of \omega_r, \phi_r, \psi_r^\epsilon. By the SVD perturbation theorem, small changes in the matrix elements produce bounded changes in singular values. A decrease in singular values below the noise-determined threshold indicates structural change. The RSVMCR integrates over frequency bands, providing robustness to measurement noise. ∎

Definition 4.3 (Towing vs. Stationary Regime). The structural signature is regime-dependent:

\mathcal{R}_t = \begin{cases} \text{Towing} & \text{if } \|a_t\| > a_{thresh} \\ \text{Stationary} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

Theorem 4.2 (Anomaly Discrimination). Let p(S_t \mid \mathcal{R}_t = \text{towing}) and p(S_t \mid \mathcal{R}_t = \text{stationary}) be the class-conditional densities. The Bayes-optimal decision boundary for anomaly detection is:

\frac{p(S_t \mid \text{degraded})}{p(S_t \mid \text{healthy})} \gtrless \frac{P(\text{healthy})}{P(\text{degraded})} \cdot \frac{C_{FP}}{C_{FN}}

where C_{FP} and C_{FN} are the costs of false positive and false negative detection.

Proof. Standard Bayes decision theory for minimum expected cost. The regime-dependent densities account for the fact that towing generates legitimate high-amplitude signatures that must not be misclassified as degradation. ∎

