6. Closed-Loop Water Systems: Model Predictive Control

Definition 6.1 (Water System State). The water state is:

W_t = [V_{fresh}, V_{grey}, Q_{quality}, F_{flow}, P_{pressure}]

Dynamics:

\dot{V}_{fresh} = -C_{consumption}(t) + R_{recovery}(t)

where R_{recovery} is the recovery rate from the greywater treatment system.

Theorem 6.1 (MPC Stability for Water Treatment). The model predictive controller for the closed-circuit reverse osmosis system is stable if the prediction horizon N_p satisfies:

N_p \geq \frac{\ln(\epsilon / \|e_0\|)}{\ln(\rho)}

where \rho < 1 is the contraction rate of the closed-loop system and \epsilon is the desired tracking error.

Proof. Standard MPC stability argument using the value function as a Lyapunov function. The prediction horizon must be long enough to capture the dominant time constant of the filtration dynamics, which is typically 3 hours for CCRO systems. ∎

Definition 6.2 (Water Autonomy Prediction). The Water Agent estimates:

T_{water} = \frac{V_{fresh}}{\hat{C}_{daily}}

where \hat{C}_{daily} is predicted consumption based on occupancy and activity.

