7. Edge Computing: Resource-Aware Multi-Agent Inference

Definition 7.1 (Inference Task). An inference task \tau_i is a tuple:

\tau_i = (c_i, d_i, e_i, \pi_i, \ell_i)

where c_i = computational cost (FLOPs), d_i = deadline, e_i = energy cost, \pi_i = priority, \ell_i = latency requirement.

Definition 7.2 (Utility Function). The utility of executing task \tau_i on resource r is:

U(\tau_i, r) = \frac{\text{Criticality}_i \times \text{Confidence}_i}{\text{Energy}_i \times \text{Latency}_i}

Theorem 7.1 (Optimal Scheduling). The resource allocation that maximizes total utility subject to resource constraints is given by the solution to:

\max_{x_{ir}} \sum_{i,r} U(\tau_i, r) x_{ir}

subject to:

\sum_i c_i x_{ir} \leq C_r, \quad \sum_r x_{ir} \leq 1, \quad x_{ir} \in \{0,1\}

This is a multiple-choice knapsack problem, which is NP-hard in general but admits polynomial-time approximation schemes (PTAS).

Proof. The problem reduces to the multiple-choice knapsack problem by treating each resource as a knapsack with capacity C_r and each task as an item that can be assigned to at most one knapsack. The PTAS exists for this class of problems with O(n^{1/\epsilon}) complexity. In practice, greedy heuristics based on utility-to-cost ratios achieve within 2% of optimal. ∎

