8. Human–AI Authority Allocation: Game-Theoretic Framework

Definition 8.1 (Authority Allocation). Let \alpha(t) \in [0,1] represent the fraction of authority allocated to the AI at time t. The human retains 1 - \alpha(t).

Definition 8.2 (Shared Control Dynamics). The system dynamics under shared control are:

\dot{x} = \alpha(t) f_{AI}(x) + (1 - \alpha(t)) f_H(x) + \omega(t)

where f_{AI} and f_H are the AI and human control policies.

Theorem 8.1 (Nash Equilibrium Authority). The optimal authority allocation \alpha^* satisfies the Nash equilibrium condition:

\alpha^* = \arg\min_{\alpha} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|x - x_{ref}\|_Q^2 + \alpha^2 R_{AI} + (1-\alpha)^2 R_H \right]

with the first-order condition:

\alpha^* = \frac{\mathbb{E}[\langle f_{AI} - f_H, x_{ref} - x \rangle_Q]}{\mathbb{E}[\|f_{AI} - f_H\|_Q^2] + R_{AI} + R_H}

Proof. The cost function is convex in \alpha. Taking the derivative with respect to \alpha and setting to zero:

\frac{\partial J}{\partial \alpha} = 2\alpha \mathbb{E}[\|f_{AI} - f_H\|_Q^2] - 2\mathbb{E}[\langle f_{AI} - f_H, x_{ref} - x \rangle_Q] + 2\alpha R_{AI} - 2(1-\alpha) R_H = 0

Solving for \alpha gives the result. The Nash equilibrium is unique because the second derivative is positive: \frac{\partial^2 J}{\partial \alpha^2} = 2(\mathbb{E}[\|f_{AI} - f_H\|_Q^2] + R_{AI} + R_H) > 0. ∎

Corollary 8.1 (Authority Bounds). The authority allocation must satisfy:

\alpha(t) \leq \alpha_{max}(\text{Criticality}(t))

where \alpha_{max} decreases with criticality. For consequential decisions (Mode III), \alpha_{max} = 0.

Definition 8.3 (Trust Dynamics). Trust evolves according to:

\dot{T} = \gamma (T_{target} - T) + \delta \cdot \mathbb{1}[\text{AI correct}]

where T_{target} is the desired trust level and \gamma is the trust adaptation rate.

