9. Digital Twin: Multi-Fidelity Gaussian Process Regression

Definition 9.1 (Digital Twin State). The digital twin maintains:

DT_t = \{\hat{X}_t, \Sigma_t, \mathcal{M}_t\}

where \hat{X}_t is the estimated state, \Sigma_t is the uncertainty covariance, and \mathcal{M}_t is the model set.

Definition 9.2 (Multi-Fidelity Surrogate). The digital twin uses multi-fidelity Gaussian process regression:

f_{high}(x) = \rho f_{low}(x) + \delta(x)

where \rho is the correlation coefficient and \delta(x) \sim \mathcal{GP}(0, k_\delta(x, x')) is the discrepancy function.

Theorem 9.1 (Predictive Accuracy Bound). The predictive variance of the multi-fidelity model is bounded by:

\sigma^2_{high}(x) \leq \sigma^2_{low}(x) + \sigma^2_\delta

with equality when \rho = 0.

Proof. The predictive variance of the sum of two Gaussian processes is \sigma^2_{high} = \rho^2 \sigma^2_{low} + \sigma^2_\delta. Since \rho^2 \leq 1, \sigma^2_{high} \leq \sigma^2_{low} + \sigma^2_\delta. ∎

Corollary 9.1 (Graceful Degradation of the Twin). When computational resources are scarce, the twin operates at lower fidelity:

DT_{reduced} = \{\hat{X}_t, \Sigma_t^{inflated}, \mathcal{M}_{low}\}

with inflated uncertainty \Sigma_t^{inflated} = \Sigma_t + \Delta, where \Delta accounts for model reduction error. The system never fails; it degrades.

