10. Modular Architecture: Graph-Theoretic Configuration Space

Definition 10.1 (Module Graph). The modular interior is represented as a labeled graph:

G = (V_M, E_M, \ell)

where V_M are module vertices, E_M are physical connection edges, and \ell: V_M \rightarrow \mathcal{T} assigns module types.

Definition 10.2 (Configuration Space). The configuration space is:

\mathcal{C} = \{G \mid G \text{ is a valid module graph}\}

Theorem 10.1 (Resource Prediction from Configuration). For a configuration G \in \mathcal{C}, the predicted resource consumption is:

\hat{P}_{total}(G) = P_{base} + \sum_{v \in V_M} P_{\ell(v)}

where P_{\ell(v)} is the nominal power draw of module type \ell(v).

Proof. By superposition of module power draws, assuming negligible interaction effects. Interaction terms (e.g., thermal coupling between modules) can be added as a correction: \hat{P}_{total} = P_{base} + \sum P_{\ell(v)} + \sum_{u,v} P_{interaction}(u,v). ∎

Corollary 10.1 (Configuration Enumeration). The number of valid configurations is:

|\mathcal{C}| = \sum_{k=1}^{|V_M|} \binom{|V_M|}{k} \cdot \mathbb{1}[\text{valid}(G_k)]

which grows combinatorially, motivating the use of graph neural networks for configuration optimization.

