Theorem 13.1 (No Single Point of Failure). If the system is designed such that:

1. Each agent has a local fallback controller,
2. The communication graph \mathcal{E}(t) is k-connected,
3. The compute fabric is redundant,

then:

P(\text{habitat failure}) \leq P(\text{cascading failure}) \cdot P(\text{all fallbacks fail})

which is bounded by the product of independent failure probabilities.

Proof. By the redundancy design, any single component failure is absorbed by the fallback. Cascading failure requires simultaneous failure of all redundant paths. If failures are independent with probability p_f, then P(\text{cascade}) = p_f^k for k-fold redundancy, which decreases exponentially with k. ∎

