Part III — Formal Proofs

Proof 1: Stability of Coupled Resource Optimization

Theorem. The coupled optimization:

\min_{\pi} J = \alpha E + \beta W + \gamma Q + \delta D + \eta R

subject to E \geq E_{min}, W \geq W_{min}, Q \in Q_{safe}, T \in T_{comfort}, S \in S_{safe}, and \text{Authority}(\pi) \leq \text{Authority}_{human} for consequential actions, converges to a stable operating point.

Proof. Define the Lyapunov function:

V(X) = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i} w_i (X_i - X_i^*)^2

where X_i^* are the optimal steady-state values. The time derivative:

\dot{V} = \sum_i w_i (X_i - X_i^*) \dot{X}_i

Substituting the dynamics \dot{X}_i = f_i(X, U) + \omega_i and the control U = -\nabla J:

\dot{V} = \sum_i w_i (X_i - X_i^*) \left[ f_i(X, -\nabla J) + \omega_i \right]

Under the convexity assumption on J and the Lipschitz continuity of f_i, there exists \kappa > 0 such that:

\dot{V} \leq -\kappa V + \sum_i w_i (X_i - X_i^*) \omega_i

Taking expectation and using \mathbb{E}[\omega_i] = 0:

\mathbb{E}[\dot{V}] \leq -\kappa \mathbb{E}[V]

which implies exponential convergence in expectation:

\mathbb{E}[V(t)] \leq V(0) e^{-\kappa t}

The authority constraint \text{Authority}(\pi) \leq \text{Authority}_{human} does not affect stability because it only restricts the admissible control set; the Lyapunov argument holds for any subset of controls that includes the equilibrium. ∎

Proof 2: Digital Twin Convergence

Theorem. The multi-fidelity digital twin converges to the true state under persistent excitation.

Proof. The digital twin update is:

\hat{X}_{t+1} = \hat{X}_t + K_t (Y_t - h(\hat{X}_t))

where K_t is the Kalman gain. The estimation error e_t = X_t - \hat{X}_t evolves as:

e_{t+1} = (I - K_t H) e_t + \omega_t - K_t \nu_t

Under the standard Kalman filter assumptions (linearity, Gaussian noise, observability), the error covariance P_t = \mathbb{E}[e_t e_t^T] converges to the steady-state solution of the Riccati equation:

P = (I - KH) P (I - KH)^T + Q + K R K^T

Since the system is observable and controllable, P_t \rightarrow P_\infty and e_t \rightarrow 0 in mean square. ∎

Proof 3: Authority Allocation Optimality

Theorem. The Nash equilibrium authority allocation \alpha^* minimizes the expected cost.

Proof. From Theorem 8.1, the cost function is:

J(\alpha) = \mathbb{E}[\|x - x_{ref}\|_Q^2] + \alpha^2 R_{AI} + (1-\alpha)^2 R_H

Taking the derivative and setting to zero:

\frac{\partial J}{\partial \alpha} = 2\alpha \mathbb{E}[\|f_{AI} - f_H\|_Q^2] - 2\mathbb{E}[\langle f_{AI} - f_H, x_{ref} - x \rangle_Q] + 2\alpha R_{AI} - 2(1-\alpha) R_H = 0

Solving:

\alpha^* = \frac{\mathbb{E}[\langle f_{AI} - f_H, x_{ref} - x \rangle_Q]}{\mathbb{E}[\|f_{AI} - f_H\|_Q^2] + R_{AI} + R_H}

The second derivative is positive:

\frac{\partial^2 J}{\partial \alpha^2} = 2(\mathbb{E}[\|f_{AI} - f_H\|_Q^2] + R_{AI} + R_H) > 0

so \alpha^* is a minimum. Since the cost is convex and the strategy space is compact, the Nash equilibrium is unique. ∎

