RQ1. How can distributed AI agents coordinate in a resource-constrained mobile habitat without creating a single point of failure?

RQ2. How can structural-health monitoring distinguish normal towing operation from progressive degradation?

RQ3. How can energy, water, thermal comfort, air quality, and computation be optimized as a coupled resource system?

RQ4. How can human authority and AI autonomy be dynamically allocated according to risk and uncertainty?

RQ5. How can perception remain useful while minimizing privacy intrusion?

RQ6. How can the digital twin operate under limited compute and intermittent connectivity?

RQ7. How can a terrestrial mobile habitat provide experimentally meaningful analogues for future extraterrestrial habitats?

H1. Distributed intelligence provides greater resilience than centralized AI.

H2. Multimodal fusion improves towing/stationary discrimination.

H3. Coupled optimization achieves lower total resource consumption than independent controllers.

H4. Adaptive authority allocation reduces unnecessary human intervention while preserving control.

H5. Resource-aware digital twin maintains useful performance under degradation.

H6. Electronically identifiable modules improve resource prediction accuracy.

